    "# Mugshot \u2192 offense-category classifier: fast pipeline + honest evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Baseline (your log):** MobileNetV3-S, best val macro-AUC **0.6931** (epoch 6, picked on the same 10 % it was reported on), ~3 min/epoch, CPU-bound.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What the analysis of `labels_utf8.csv` found (numbers are from the uploaded file)\n",
    "* **The file is not the one in your log:** 69,418 rows here vs 69,827 in the log (255,028 vs 255,597 charges). Check that Kaggle and this notebook use the same version.\n",
    "* **Parsing bug:** offenses are comma-split, but charge names contain thousands separators (`MANU/DEL 15,000 GRAMS`), producing junk fragments (`000 GRAMS`, `000`, `5`, `Not Available`). Fixed, plus a few missed spellings (`CONT. SUBS`, `DEL SUB`, `FAILURE TO REPORT`, `CRIMINAL SEX ASSAULT`, \u2026): unrecognised charge instances go from **3.7 % (your log) \u2192 0.9 %**. Inmates whose charges are *all* unrecognised are now dropped instead of being trained as all-negative.\n",
    "* **Prevalence is per-charge in your log, per-inmate here.** Inmate level (v1 categories): Drug 39 %, Property 36 %, Weapon 35 %, Assault 27 %, Robbery 20 %, Traffic_Other 19 %, Homicide 16 %, Sexual 11 %, **Kidnapping 2.8 %** (~190 positives in a 10 % test split \u2192 wide AUC CI).\n",
    "* **Label double-counting:** `ARMED ROBBERY` (\u224811k charges) is tagged Robbery *and* Weapon, so P(Weapon | Robbery) = 0.70; sexual-assault strings also hit Assault; `POSS ELEC CONTRA PENAL INST` (prison contraband) is tagged Drug. About 45 % of the Kidnapping charges are (aggravated) `UNLAWFUL RESTRAINT`, and the category is only 2.8 % of inmates. `Traffic_Other` is a grab-bag (DUI, escape, obstruction, registration violations).\n",
    "* **Label count tracks record length** (corr(#charges, #labels) = 0.56): anything that predicts \"long record\" lifts *every* category.\n",
    "* **Scheme `v2` (7 targets):** Kidnapping merged into Robbery; Weapon = standalone weapon possession/use only; Assault excludes sexual assault; `Traffic_Other` dropped as a target (still recognised so all-\"other\" inmates are valid negatives). Sexual_Offenses is kept in both schemes and also receives the sex-offender-registry charges.\n",
    "\n",
    "## The important caveat: how much of 0.69 is a face?\n",
    "Reference models that **never see a face** (gradient boosting on tabular columns, same labels, 90/10 split like your baseline):\n",
    "\n",
    "| features | macro AUC |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| sex, race, hair, eyes | 0.596 |\n",
    "| + weight, height | 0.609 |\n",
    "\n",
    "Your CNN's 0.693 and every later result are well above these, so the photos carry more than those columns. But apparent age, apparent weight, photo era and camera setup are all visible in a mugshot and plausibly correlate with charge type, and the data has no age or date column to control for them. So a decent score is **not evidence that faces encode offenses**. `report.py` prints the tabular references next to the model and also reports AUC **within race \u00d7 sex strata**. (An earlier version also showed a baseline built from the ID. That was removed from the default report: the ID is an arbitrary key, never a model input, and not something a photo contains. `report.py --id_baseline` shows it as a clearly labelled diagnostic only.)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Plan / how to run (top to bottom)\n",
    "1. `labels.py` (shared label code; **never reads the sex-offender column**) \u2192 2. `build_cache.py` (decode JPEGs once \u2192 uint8 memmap) \u2192 3. sanity checks (loader speed, 1-minute smoke test) \u2192 4. `probe.py` (rank backbones with frozen features in minutes) \u2192 5. `train.py` under DDP on both T4s \u2192 6. `report.py` (per-category AUC + CIs, ensemble, confound check)."
   ]
