    "## Caveats\n",
    "* **Reliability.** Test split = 10 % (~6.9k inmates); per-category CIs are in the report (Sexual \u2248 800 and Robbery_Kidnap \u2248 1.5k positives; the old Kidnapping category had \u2248 190). Differences under roughly \u00b10.01 macro-AUC between runs are within noise, and one seed per config was run. Model selection uses val; only the test split is reported.\n",
    "* **The ID.** The `ID` column is used only to line the image cache up with the CSV (an assert in `train.py`). The model sees pixels only. Note that any readable ID/inmate number or date in the photo itself (placards) could still leak; I could not view the images.\n",
    "* **Background removal.** The matting model can be wrong (check the previews), the grey fill is out-of-distribution for a model trained with backgrounds, and the person's silhouette and the mask edge can still carry information. Clothing was deliberately not analysed.\n",
    "* **Possible duplicates.** The CSV has no person identifier; if the same person appears under several IDs, train/test leakage is possible and cannot be checked from this file.\n",
    "* **Photo artefacts.** Backgrounds, height rulers, placards, lighting and camera type differ by era and facility. I could not view the images; `build_cache.py --crop 0.8` (centre crop) is one cheap test of whether border content matters, and `gray_p` already randomises colour.\n",
    "* **What the numbers mean.** Even a high within-strata AUC would show that something in the photo predicts charge type, not that faces reveal offending; apparent age, weight, tattoos, grooming and era remain. This model should not be used to assess any individual.\n",
    "* **Sex-offender flag vs. sexual-offense labels.** The `Sex Offender` *column* is never loaded and is not a model input. Sexual_Offenses remains a target, and registry-type *charges* (`VIOLATE SEX OFFENDER REGIS`, `FAILURE TO REPORT ANNUALLY/2+` (my assumption that this one is registry-related), `CHILD SEX OFFENDER/SCHOOL ZONE`) are labelled Sexual_Offenses (`SEXREG_AS_SEXUAL` in `labels.py`; set it to False to move them to `Other`). Note these are compliance/status charges rather than sexual acts, so they may be a different kind of signal."
